WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. Today's video is about weird things that can happen.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.920
So I went to Kenyon College in Gambier, Ohio.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.300
Kenyon is like Hogwarts, except with fewer Death Eaters and more corn.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.400
I was an undistinguished student, but I was smart enough to surround myself with very smart people

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.800
like Bruce, with whom I would eventually spend a disastrous summer in Moose Pass, Alaska,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.600
And Mary Fran, who would later get married inside an elephant.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.360
So Mary Fran and Bruce decided they wanted to read James Joyce's

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:27.620
'Ulysses' together for an independent study and asked me if I would join them, and since I liked the professor and also figured a class

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.460
about a single book couldn't be that hard,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.840
I agreed. We were joined by a precocious sophomore named Randy, who was taking this picture

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.060
so needs to be photoshopped in—perfect—

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.640
and then together we learned just how hard a class about a single book could be.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.520
Hank, I spent more time on my final paper in that class than I did on anything else in college, except possibly trying to get girls

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.529
to make out with me. And I remember everybody's paper:

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.789
I wrote about nationalism, Mary Fran wrote about Molly Bloom, Bruce wrote about this potato

00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:56.780
that was inside Bloom's pocket for much of the book, and Randy wrote about religious deconstructionism, I don't know, it was very complicated.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:00.400
We all became very close in that class, and it was the first time I ever felt academically grown up.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.000
Like, this was back in the days when I thought that adulthood would basically consist of having a series of very interesting

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.000
conversations about great books. It turns out that adulthood primarily consists of standing in line and being on hold, but yeah.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.740
So I graduated from college thinking I would become an Episcopal minister,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.080
Mary Fran went off to law school, Randy went off to film school, and Bruce went off to become a musician.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.100
After I decided not to go to divinity school, I got some writing jobs, including one working for Mental Floss,

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.019
And then I was in a position where I had to hire a freelance writer

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.460
And I remembered that Randy was pretty good at, you know, explaining things simply, so I hired him.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.479
But he still wanted to make movies.

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:32.470
I left Mental Floss for the world of young adult novels,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:39.640
Bruce now reports for the NPR show 'The World', and Mary Fran, having become a lawyer, heroically abandoned the law in favor of

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.800
librarianship. God I love librarians. All the while, Randy

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.060
and I stayed friends, and eventually he started uploading his weird little

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.960
documentaries to YouTube, where they became extremely popular because they are amazing.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.320
And then one day he told me he was working on a novel based on thousands of found photographs he's collected.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.420
By then, Randy Riggs was going by his real name, Ransom Riggs,

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:59.519
and he sent me his finished book, Miss Peregrine's Home for Peculiar Children,

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.360
and I thought that it was weird and wonderful and quite unlike anything I had ever read.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.220
But even so, Hank, I kept trying to lower his expectations because a lot of books come out every week. By then

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:11.369
I had published four novels of my own, and a couple

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:16.530
of them made it to the New York Times bestseller list, but two of them had sold less than a hundred copies in their first

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.890
weeks in print. I've seen great books get lost in crowded bookstores over and over again,

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.719
and I kept telling Ransom it's a marathon

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.820
not a sprint, your first novel isn't your last novel, blah blah blah blah blah blah bl

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.260
It was an instant bestseller. A few months later The Fault in Our Stars came out, and next week,

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.840
amazingly, our books will be #1 and #2 on the New York Times bestseller list.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.459
So Hank, that's nice and funny and weird,

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:39.869
but it's not the real story. Professional lives, which take turns you can never imagine, from lawyer to

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.899
librarian, or from minister to vlogger, are important.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.879
But it's not what life is about. Human life is really about relationships and communities. When I think of my friendship with Randy,

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:52.289
I don't think of this, although it's cool—I think of us driving to the Grand Canyon with our friend Kathy.

00:02:52.289 --> 00:02:55.760
I think of driving through the snow in Ohio and the endlessness of Texas

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.590
and watching the sunrise creep up the walls of the Grand Canyon.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.480
"You takin' pictures of me dude?"

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.760
And then I think of us twelve years later, walking around Los Angeles together, still taking pictures. What a blessing.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.080
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

